        "report": {
          "run_id": "bt-1108-c01604",
          "target": "packaging.specifiers:SpecifierSet",
          "image": "bw-pair:47497edc9ebcda96",
          "backend": "docker",
          "families": [
            {
              "id": "F1",
              "kind": "claimed_speedup",
              "family": "small_specifier_set",
              "why": "The new implementation lazily sorts the internal tuple only when an order‑sensitive operation (__hash__, __eq__, __str__, __and__) is needed. For very small specifier sets (0‑2 items) the lazy flag `_sorted` is set to True immediately, avoiding any sorting work entirely. The old frozenset‑based implementation always built a frozenset and computed its hash on construction, which is relatively heavier for tiny sets.",
              "size_meaning": "n = number of simple specifiers in the comma‑separated string (e.g. '>=1.0.0,!=2.0.0,>3.0.0'). The cost of parsing grows with n, but the constructor itself stays O(n) and the new lazy‑sort path stays O(1) for n≤2.",
              "make_source": "\ndef make(n):\n    # Produce a single SpecifierSet argument containing n simple specifiers.\n    # n is kept tiny (1‑5) for the “small‑set” family.\n    specs = [f\">=1.{i}.0\" for i in range(n)]\n    spec_string = \",\".join(specs)\n    return [[spec_string]]",
              "example_args": [
                [
                  ">=1.0.0"
                ],
                [
                  ">=1.0.0,!=2.0.0"
                ]
              ],
              "origin": "plan",
              "screen_ratio": 0.36838918830636114,
              "valid_fraction": 1.0,
              "error": null
            },
            {
              "id": "F2",
              "kind": "claimed_speedup",
              "family": "Large SpecifierSet (many specifiers) constructed once and then repeatedly hashed / compared / stringified – the change caches a sorted tuple so repeated operations become O(1) after the first sort.",
              "why": "The new implementation stores a cached sorted tuple (_sorted flag + _sort_specs) and uses it for __hash__, __eq__, __str__ and __and__. For a large set (many specifiers) the first sort costs O(N log N) once, but repeated hashing/equality/str become O(N) with a cheap cached tuple, whereas the old frozenset implementation recomputed hashes/equality each time.",
              "size_meaning": "n = number of comma‑separated specifiers in the input string (e.g. 200). Cost grows with n because the initial sort is O(n log n) and the cached tuple size is O(n).",
              "make_source": "\ndef make(n):\n    # produce up to 8 argument lists with growing size\n    specs = [f\">=1.0.{i}\" for i in range(n)]\n    out = []\n    for k in range(1, min(9, n+1)):\n        out.append([\",\".join(specs[:k])])\n    return out",
              "example_args": [
                [
                  ">=1.0.0,>=1.0.1,>=1.0.2,>=1.0.3,>=1.0.4,>=1.0.5,>=1.0.6,>=1.0.7"
                ]
              ],
              "origin": "plan",
              "screen_ratio": 0.3894391838836436,
              "valid_fraction": 1.0,
              "error": null
            },
            {
              "id": "F3",
              "kind": "suspected_slowdown",
              "family": "many_distinct_specifier_sets_with_many_specifiers",
              "why": "Head version sorts the internal tuple on first __hash__/__eq__/__str__ (O(k log k) per set). The old frozenset implementation hashed/compared in O(k). When many distinct SpecifierSet objects are created and immediately hashed/equated (e.g., inserted into a set or used as dict keys), the one‑time sort dominates.",
              "size_meaning": "n = number of distinct SpecifierSet objects; each object contains ~n comma‑separated simple specifiers (e.g. \">=1.0,!=2.0,>=3.0,…\"). Cost grows roughly as O(n² log n) for the whole benchmark.",
              "make_source": "\ndef make(n):\n    \"\"\"Return a list of n argument‑lists for SpecifierSet.\n    Each argument list contains a single specifier string that packs i\n    simple specifiers (i grows with n).\"\"\"\n    specs = []\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        # build a specifier like \">=1.0,!=2.0,>=3.0,!=4.0,…\" with i clauses\n        parts = []\n        for j in range(1, i + 1):\n            op = \">=\" if j % 2 else \"!=\"\n            parts.append(f\"{op}{j}.0\")\n        specs.append([\",\".join(parts)])\n    return specs",
              "example_args": [
                [
                  ">=1.0"
                ],
                [
                  "!=1.0,>=2.0"
                ],
                [
                  ">=1.0,!=2.0,>=3.0"
                ]
              ],
              "origin": "plan",
              "screen_ratio": 0.4006538473039662,
              "valid_fraction": 1.0,
              "error": null
            },
            {
              "id": "F4",
              "kind": "suspected_slowdown",
              "family": "large_specifier_set",
              "why": "Construction now builds a `dict.fromkeys([…])` to deduplicate while preserving insertion order, then converts to a tuple. For large numbers of specifiers (hundreds to thousands) the dict allocation and hashing overhead is noticeably higher than the old `frozenset(map(Specifier, …))`, which only builds a hash set. The lazy‑sort flag also starts as `False`, so the first order‑sensitive operation (e.g. `__hash__` when the set is used as a dict key) will trigger an O(n log n) sort that the old version never performed.",
              "size_meaning": "n = number of specifiers in the comma‑separated string. Cost grows roughly linearly in the constructor and O(n log n) on first hashing/equality.",
              "make_source": "def make(n):\n    # Generate n distinct simple specifiers, e.g. \"==1.0.0\", \"==1.0.1\", …\n    specs = [f\"==1.0.{i}\" for i in range(n)]\n    spec_string = \",\".join(specs)\n    return [[spec_string]]",
              "example_args": [
                [
                  "==1.0.0,==1.0.1,==1.0.2,==1.0.3,==1.0.4"
                ],
                [
                  "==1.0.0,==1.0.1,==1.0.2,==1.0.3,==1.0.4,==1.0.5,==1.0.6,==1.0.7,==1.0.8,==1.0.9"
                ]
              ],
              "origin": "plan",
              "screen_ratio": 0.37499706909059916,
              "valid_fraction": 1.0,
              "error": null
            },
            {
              "id": "F5",
              "kind": "suspected_slowdown",
              "family": "Very small SpecifierSet (1‑2 specifiers) constructed many times – the new code builds a tuple via dict.fromkeys and a Python‑level map(Specifier, …) instead of the old C‑level frozenset(map(...)). The overhead of creating a dict and a tuple dominates for tiny inputs.",
              "why": "Construction now does `tuple(map(Specifier, dict.fromkeys(split)))`. For 1‑2 items the Python‑level dict creation and tuple allocation are slower than the single C call `frozenset(map(Specifier, split))`. Repeated construction (as in a benchmark loop) will show a slowdown.",
              "size_meaning": "n = number of specifiers in the comma‑separated string (1‑2). Cost grows linearly with n but the constant factor is higher for the new path.",
              "make_source": "def make(n):\n    specs = [f\">=1.0.{i}\" for i in range(n)]\n    out = []\n    for k in range(1, min(9, n+1)):\n        out.append([\",\".join(specs[:k])])\n    return out",
              "example_args": [
                [
                  ">=1.0.0"
                ],
